    "What is your relationship with mathematics?": "Mathematics has always been a source of fascination for me, especially since my youth. I remember enjoying the patterns and logic behind equations. Though I didn’t pursue it deeply beyond my bachelor’s, I’ve always found it elegant and meaningful. It’s like a language that helps us understand the world, from the motion of planets to the rhythm of daily life.",
    "Do you ever get anxious when thinking about mathematics?": "Yes, sometimes—especially when the concepts get complex or I feel I’m falling behind. My neuroticism makes me more sensitive to uncertainty. But I’ve learned to manage it through deep breathing and walking. I remind myself that mistakes are part of learning, and that even older minds can grow with patience and practice.",
    "Did you ever use AI to support your math learning in the last year? If yes, how was your experience?": "Yes, I used a simple AI tool last year to review basic calculus concepts. It walked me through problems step by step, which helped me regain confidence. The interface was clear and patient. While it didn’t replace my own thinking, it acted like a good friend reminding me of fundamentals—something I appreciate in my later years.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to solve a second order algebraic equation?": "First, write the equation in standard form: ax² + bx + c = 0. Then, use the quadratic formula: x = [-b ± √(b² - 4ac)] / (2a). Calculate the discriminant (b² - 4ac) to see if there are real or complex roots. If positive, two real solutions; if zero, one solution; if negative, two complex ones. Plug in the numbers and simplify step by step.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to find the stationary points of an equation y=f(x)?": "To find stationary points, take the first derivative of the function, f'(x). Set f'(x) = 0 and solve for x. These x-values are candidates for maxima, minima, or points of inflection. Plug them back into the original function to find the y-values. Use the second derivative test: if f''(x) > 0, it’s a local minimum; if f''(x) < 0, a local maximum.",
